Back at WWDC 2025, Apple revealed that it was planning to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/06/12/carplay-airplay-video-in-the-car/">allow CarPlay users to watch video via AirPlay in their vehicles</a> while they are not driving, and the first beta of iOS 26.4 suggests the feature may be nearing availability.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/02/Apple-CarPlay-iOS-26-Tight-Crop.jpg" alt="" width="2880" height="1620" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1034782" /><br/>
The original mention of the feature appeared on <a href="https://developer.apple.com/carplay/">Apple's CarPlay page for developers</a> within a section specific to automakers, indicating that <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/carplay/">CarPlay</a> with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/airplay/">AirPlay</a> video is something automakers will need to implement with on their end. Presumably, this is because there needs to be integration with vehicle systems to ensure that the feature can only be used while the vehicle is parked so as to hinder the potential for distracted driving.<br/>
<br/>
The first iOS 26.4 beta seeded to developers today includes a number of new references to &zwnj;CarPlay&zwnj; video playback, including features like onboarding flows, notifications, and more.<br/>
<br/>
Unsurprisingly, it appears that <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/apple-tv/">Apple TV</a> will likely support the &zwnj;CarPlay&zwnj; video feature when it launches, as there are additional references to interacting with the TV app while using &zwnj;CarPlay&zwnj;, such as a message that would pop up informing users that they will need to subscribe to &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; on their <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/iphone/">iPhone</a> while not driving if they attempt to access subscription content.<br/>
<br/>
